---

Two programs pass each other unforgeable references to objects and call methods
across the network, under the same rule as local code. Hold the reference and
you can use it; if you do not, there is nothing to forge. That is
[OCapN](https://github.com/ocapn/ocapn). pancake is a small
[Zig](https://ziglang.org/) library that speaks it.

[Their program hands yours a reference to an object called "greeter." Holding that reference is the whole permission to call it.]($image.siteAsset('diagram-capability.svg'))

The protocol core is deterministic, synchronous, and does no I/O. Sockets and
scheduling live in adapters above it. Frame ownership, copies, and wire format
stay visible at the boundary that owns them.

Peers can meet without a server in the middle: the
[iroh transport]($link.page('transports/iroh')) carries CapTP over encrypted QUIC
and names each peer by a self-authenticating key. It is an optional artifact: a
TCP-only program never pays for it.

Hold the library as a Zig package, a plain C ABI, or a native binding (Rust,
TypeScript, Python, Racket). An installable [CapTP CLI]($link.page('cli')) sits
beside those surfaces (`zig build pancake`).

This site is the front door. Deep living-state docs live
[next to the source](https://codeberg.org/jimisaacs/pancake/src/branch/main/docs).
New to the ideas? [Concepts]($link.page('concepts')). Ready to dial?
[Start building]($link.page('start')).
